        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 22,
        "headingId": 1089,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:08:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1074,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 1090,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151325,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 22,
        "headingId": 1090,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:08:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1075,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1091,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151326,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 22,
        "headingId": 1091,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Bartholin gland carcinomas arise in the region of the Bartholin glands (bilateral symmetrically located glands in the posterolateral introitus).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:08:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1076,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1092,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151327,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 22,
        "headingId": 1092,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Bartholin gland carcinomas present as a painless mass and may be confused with a Bartholin gland cyst or abscess.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:08:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1077,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1093,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151328,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 22,
        "headingId": 1093,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are rare tumours, accounting for &lt;&nbsp;1% of vulvar neoplasms in different case series and typically occur in middle-aged to elderly women {29406447; 26195958}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:08:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1078,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1094,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151329,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 22,
        "headingId": 1094,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:08:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1079,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1095,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151330,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 22,
        "headingId": 1095,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most squamous cell carcinomas of the Bartholin gland are high-risk HPV-associated neoplasms {29406447}. Adenoid cystic carcinomas may be associated with <em>NFIB</em>-associated gene rearrangement {27662035}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:08:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1086,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1102,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151331,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 22,
        "headingId": 1102,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Bartholin gland carcinomas form a mass of variable size that may be ulcerated or may be deep to the surface with intact overlying skin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:08:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1080,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1096,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151332,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 22,
        "headingId": 1096,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The following criteria have been proposed for a tumour to be considered a Bartholin gland primary: (1)&nbsp;the tumour should involve the anatomical region of the Bartholin gland; (2)&nbsp;the tumour should be histologically compatible with an origin in the Bartholin gland; (3)&nbsp;an area of transition should be present between benign Bartholin gland and tumour; and (4)&nbsp;there should be no evidence of an alternative primary {29406447}. However, in practice, not all of these criteria are fulfilled in each case.</p>\r\n<p>Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common morphological type, followed by adenocarcinoma. More uncommon histological types include poorly differentiated carcinoma, adenosquamous carcinoma, neuroendocrine carcinoma (NEC), and a variety of salivary gland&ndash;type carcinomas (including adenoid cystic carcinoma, myoepithelial carcinoma, and epithelial&ndash;myoepithelial carcinoma) {27662035; 19620948; 16194566}. The squamous cell carcinomas are usually poorly differentiated, with a somewhat basaloid morphology, and papillary and transitional-like patterns have been described; they typically exhibit diffuse block-type immunoreactivity for p16, presumably secondary to high-risk HPV infection {29406447}. Adenocarcinomas are of variable differentiation and may be of mucinous type or have a papillary architecture. Occasional cases of Bartholin gland adenocarcinoma associated with vulvar extramammary Paget disease have been reported {2845708}. The morphological features of the other neoplasms are identical to those seen when these neoplasms occur at other sites.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:08:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1081,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1097,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151333,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 22,
        "headingId": 1097,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:08:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1082,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 1098,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151334,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 22,
        "headingId": 1098,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:08:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1085,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 1101,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151335,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 22,
        "headingId": 1101,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential</em><em>:</em> involvement of the anatomical region of the Bartholin gland; histology compatible with an origin in the Bartholin gland.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable</em><em>:</em> adjacent non-neoplastic Bartholin gland.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:08:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1083,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 1099,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151336,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 22,
        "headingId": 1099,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These neoplasms are staged using the FIGO and TNM staging systems.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:08:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1084,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 1100,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151337,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 22,
        "headingId": 1100,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Prognosis is largely dependent on the stage at diagnosis. Ipsilateral inguinofemoral lymph node metastasis is identified at presentation in approximately 20% of cases. In one series, the 5-year survival rate was 67%, with 54.5% of patients experiencing recurrence during a mean follow-up time of 73.5&nbsp;months {11531274}. In another study, it was concluded that most tumours present at an early stage and are associated with relatively favourable outcomes {29406447}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:08:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]